  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Vigor Marine has $685M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Vigor Marine won 25 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $27.4M and obligations up 54.3% year over year. Vigor Marine’s FY26 obligations are larger than any prior year listed, topping FY25 obligations of $444M and FY24 obligations of $448M. Vigor Marine’s work is concentrated in a small set of buyers and markets. The Department of Defense obligated $654M to Vigor Marine in FY2026 to date, while the Department of Homeland Security obligated $30.4M and the Department of Transportation obligated $860K. Ship Building and Repairing is the dominant NAICS market for Vigor Marine, with $684M in obligations, followed by Other Support Activities for Water Transportation at $860K and Steam and Air-Conditioning Supply at $2.8K. This snapshot covers fiscal year to date figures and notes that some award-value figures in award and expiring-contract tables represent total contract values rather than obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Vigor Marine’s biggest federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is Vigor Marine’s largest buyer, having obligated $654M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Transportation follow, with $30.4M and $860K respectively. The top buyers account for nearly all FY2026 obligations for Vigor Marine."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets does Vigor Marine serve for federal work?",
        "body": "Ship Building and Repairing is Vigor Marine’s primary market, with $684M in FY2026 to date. Other Support Activities for Water Transportation and Steam and Air-Conditioning Supply are much smaller markets, with $860K and $2.8K respectively. The market mix matches the agency mix, driven by defense shipbuilding and repair work."
      }
    ]
  },
